    "body": "<p>A river soundscape is built on moving water. Rapids and riffles produce broad, steady, white-noise-like geophony that fills the low and middle bands, waterfalls add a roaring low-frequency component, and quiet pools let biophony through: dippers call from mid-stream rocks, kingfishers rattle along the bank, frogs call from backwaters, and insects hum over the surface. The balance between water noise and animal sound shifts with flow: in spate, after rain, the river drowns nearly everything, while in low summer flow, the biophony emerges clearly. River soundscapes matter to the animals themselves: species that live beside noisy water, such as torrent ducks and some frogs, have evolved calls pitched above the water&#x27;s dominant band or timed into the gaps between surges, a neat example of acoustic niche adaptation to geophony.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: river soundscape studies measure water-noise spectra at different flows and show that animal call detection falls as flow rises, confirming masking by geophony. Species beside fast water are documented calling at higher frequencies or with altered timing compared with relatives beside quiet water, consistent with adaptation to the water-noise band. River acoustic monitoring is used to estimate discharge and to track riparian species.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if river noise measures 65 dB and a bird call measures 60 dB in the same band, the call is 5 dB below the noise, and the signal-to-noise ratio is 60 - 65 = -5 dB, meaning the call is masked. The demo prints signal-to-noise -5 dB, and for a louder 70 dB call, signal-to-noise 5 dB, plus the combined level of river and 70 dB call, 71.19 dB.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published river soundscape and torrent-species bioacoustics literature; decibel arithmetic, as published October 2026</p>",
